{"conjecture": "In any long-lived software system, each locally correct change adds at least one edge to a hidden graph of cross-module invariants, and the diameter of this graph grows as O(n log n) rather than O(n), so the number of modules that must be re-verified after a change scales with the invariant-graph diameter and therefore grows superlinearly in system size. This means the cost of a single modification rises not because any individual change is wrong, but because the reachable set of implicitly affected invariants expands faster than the module count itself, creating an asymptotic verification burden that no local review or test can bound."}
